   "id": "events",
   "plain": "You can ask for a new decision with form SSA-44 if your income went down because of a life-changing event: marriage, divorce or annulment, or a spouse's death; you or your spouse stopping or reducing work; losing income-producing property through no choice of your own, for example in a disaster or through arson, fraud or theft; a pension plan ending, being cut or reorganized; or an employer settlement after closure or bankruptcy.",
   "source": "https://www.ssa.gov/forms/ssa-44.pdf",
   "effective_from": null,
   "checked_on": "2026-11-23"
  },
  {
   "id": "proof",
   "plain": "Send proof of the event and of the lower income, such as a death certificate or a letter from your employer, and a signed copy of your tax return if you filed one.",
   "source": "https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/medicare/medicare-premiums.html",
   "effective_from": null,
   "checked_on": "2026-11-23"
  },
  {
   "id": "appeal",
   "plain": "If the information Social Security used is wrong, give it the correct information; you do not need to appeal. To appeal the decision itself, ask for reconsideration (form SSA-561-U2). If the IRS figure is wrong, it must be corrected with the IRS.",
   "source": "https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/medicare/medicare-premiums.html",
   "effective_from": null,
   "checked_on": "2026-11-23"
  },
  {
